Q: Read Outputinformation of ffmpeg-process ran from java Runtime.exec(...) A: <p>I got the solution: Use ProcesssBuilder and StringBuilder!</p>

<pre><code>Process processDuration = new ProcessBuilder("ffmpeg", "-i", absolutePath).redirectErrorStream(true).start();
StringBuilder strBuild = new StringBuilder();
try (BufferedReader processOutputReader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(processDuration.getInputStream(), Charset.defaultCharset()));) {
    String line;
    while ((line = processOutputReader.readLine()) != null) {
        strBuild.append(line + System.lineSeparator());
    }
    processDuration.waitFor();
}
String outputJson = strBuild.toString().trim();
</code></pre>

<p>it works fine for me and I'm glad to share this with everyone in the Internet :)
I got the solution from <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/8149828/read-the-output-from-java-exec" title="here">here</a>.</p>

Q: Is fixing child.prototype.constructor necessary in ES5 if we solely rely on [[Prototype]] check methods like Object.getPrototypeOf()? A: <p>Since reliable/trustable type-checking already has been mentioned at this thread, what approach can you think of that properly checks the type of a foreign-realm instances. The <code>instanceof</code> operator is of no use for such objects. And everything that relies on <code>Object.prototype.toString.call(value)</code> can be spoofed via <code>Symbol.toStringTag</code>.</p>
<p>But one always can make use of <code>Object.getPrototypeOf(foreignRealmObject)</code> and this prototype-object happens to have OWN property-descriptors, and one of them refers to the constructor. And the latter has own property-descriptors as well, and one of them refers to the constructor's name.</p>
<p>Thus a throw-safe query (yes, direct property access like <code>value.constructor</code> or <code>functionType.name</code> can be spoofed/hijacked as well by e.g., property-accessor proxy-traps) for a given value's type via its prototype and constructor-name looks like that ...</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(
  Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(
    Object.getPrototypeOf(foreignRealmObject) ?? Object.create(null),
    'constructor',
  )?.value ?? Object.create(null),
  'name',
)?.value;
</code></pre>
<p>This is just one arm, but the fastest, for checking on a foreign-realm object's type. But it relies on a properly established prototype-chain and the correctly referenced constructor. The second arm is a full structural check on the prototype chain and the validation of the correctly implemented (behavioral) contract/s of a known and/or expected type.</p>
<p>There is a reason why one should follow the path of providing an honest prototype-chain.</p>
<p>And the <code>Animal</code>/<code>Dog</code> example by @Davi demonstrates the importance. If one does convert  this very example-code into class-syntax based code, one gets the correct constructor-references within the prototype-chain for free ...</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const hazel = new Dog('Hazel');
const buddy = new Dog('Buddy');

console.log({
  hazel: {
    name: hazel.name,
    species: hazel.species,
  },
  buddy: {
    name: buddy.name,
    species: buddy.species,
  },
});
console.log(
  Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors(
    Object.getPrototypeOf(hazel),
  ),
);
console.log(
  Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors(
    Object.getPrototypeOf(
      Object.getPrototypeOf(buddy),
    ),
  ),
);</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
class Animal {
  #species;

  constructor(species) {
    this.#species = species;
  }
  get species() {
    return this.#species;
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  #name;

  constructor(name) {
    super('Dog');

    this.#name = name;
  }
  get name() {
    return this.#name;
  }
}
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: how to download portion of video which was uploaded into AWS s3 bucket, though Nodejs SDKs A: <p>You don't need to download the whole file, and you also don't need to guess byte offsets yourself. Two options:</p>
<p><strong>Option 1: let ffmpeg do the range requests.</strong> ffmpeg's HTTPS input seeks with byte ranges on its own, so point it at a presigned URL and it only fetches what it needs:</p>
<pre><code>const url = await getSignedUrl(s3, new GetObjectCommand({ Bucket, Key }), { expiresIn: 60 });
execFile(&quot;ffmpeg&quot;, [&quot;-ss&quot;, &quot;6&quot;, &quot;-i&quot;, url, &quot;-frames:v&quot;, &quot;1&quot;, &quot;-q:v&quot;, &quot;2&quot;, &quot;thumb.jpg&quot;]);
</code></pre>
<p>This is the simplest and usually fetches a few MB of a 1 GB file.</p>
<p><strong>Option 2: fetch a leading byte range with the SDK</strong> and run ffmpeg on that partial file:</p>
<pre><code>const res = await s3.send(new GetObjectCommand({ Bucket, Key, Range: &quot;bytes=0-10485759&quot; }));
</code></pre>
<p>Caveat: this only works if the mp4's <code>moov</code> atom (the index) is at the front. Upload with <code>ffmpeg -movflags +faststart</code> to guarantee that; otherwise the index sits at the end of the file and the first 10 MB are undecodable, which is why Option 1 is the safer default.</p>

Q: Update Change Password Page For Default And Other Groups A: <p>So after some search, I did it successfully.</p>
<p>Giving solution for others who may needed it</p>
<p>in catalog/controller/account/password.php added</p>
<pre><code>if ($this-&gt;customer-&gt;isLogged()) {
       $data['customer_group_id'] = $this-&gt;customer-&gt;getGroupId();
  } elseif ($this-&gt;config-&gt;get('config_customer_group_id')) {
       $data['customer_group_id'] = $this-&gt;config-&gt;get('config_customer_group_id');
  }
</code></pre>
<p>after line 12 i.e.</p>
<pre><code>  $this-&gt;load-&gt;language('account/password');
</code></pre>
